(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-asthma-quiz"]={"title":"Asthma Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 6","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Patients with asthma have the most difficulty with which part of breathing?","choices":["Getting air out of the lungs","Drawing air into the lungs","Moving oxygen across the alveolar wall","Coordinating the diaphragm with the chest wall"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Asthma makes expiration difficult; air is trapped behind the narrowed airways and the lungs hyperinflate. That is why the history asks whether symptoms come with breathing in or out, and why a prolonged expiratory phase tracks the obstruction. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 5, 45"},{"q":"Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis is best described as which process?","choices":["Invasive fungal infection of healthy lungs","Bacterial pneumonia after influenza","Occupational reaction to workplace chemicals","Hypersensitivity reaction to Aspergillus species"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis is a hypersensitivity reaction to Aspergillus species, mainly Aspergillus fumigatus, occurring almost exclusively in asthma or cystic fibrosis; exposure provokes a vigorous immunoglobulin E and G response and acute asthma symptoms. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Occupational asthma classically shows which pattern?","choices":["Symptoms peak in a monthly cycle","Symptoms improve on weekends and vacations","Symptoms worsen on weekends at home","Sensitization requires pre-existing atopy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Occupational sensitizers, mostly chemicals, cause asthma independent of atopy, and symptoms improve during weekends and vacations away from the workplace. Onset may come weeks or years after exposure and sensitization. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 30, 36"},{"q":"Which findings are common in intrinsic asthma?","choices":["Nasal polyps and aspirin sensitivity","Elevated immunoglobulin E and positive skin tests","Mild symptoms that remit in adolescence","Symptoms tied to the menstrual cycle"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Intrinsic asthma is of later onset, usually more severe and persistent, commonly has nasal polyps, and may be aspirin sensitive. Its allergy tests are negative with a normal immunoglobulin E. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Why must allergen immunotherapy for asthma be given in a controlled environment?","choices":["It must be infused over several hours","It causes oral candidiasis","It works best with many allergens at once","It can induce bronchoconstriction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Immunotherapy may be considered for patients with allergic triggers and reduces symptoms, most usefully as single-allergen therapy, but it must be given in a controlled environment because it risks inducing bronchoconstriction that may need urgent treatment. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"What is the preferred first-line long-term treatment for persistent asthma?","choices":["Theophylline","A leukotriene modifier alone","An inhaled corticosteroid","A long-acting beta-2 agonist alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Inhaled corticosteroids are the preferred first-line treatment for persistent asthma; most patients respond to twice-daily use, though the maximum response may take months. They reduce the airway inflammation that defines the disease. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which medications act as asthma triggers and should be avoided in patients with asthma?","choices":["Albuterol and levalbuterol","Aspirin and beta blockers","Inhaled corticosteroids and leukotriene modifiers","Celecoxib and meloxicam"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Aspirin and beta blockers are the medication triggers listed and should be avoided in patients with asthma; exercise is listed with them as a trigger. Patients with true aspirin sensitivity should also avoid non-selective anti-inflammatory drugs. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Daily symptoms, daily rescue inhaler use, and some limitation of activity fit which severity class?","choices":["Severe persistent","Moderate persistent","Intermittent","Mild persistent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Daily symptoms, daily short-acting beta-2 agonist use, night awakenings more than once a week but not nightly, some limitation, and a forced expiratory volume in 1 second between 60% and 80% predicted define moderate persistent asthma. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"How long can the maximum response to an inhaled corticosteroid take?","choices":["Hours","Days","Months","Minutes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Most patients respond to twice-daily inhaled corticosteroid dosing, but the maximum response may take months, so patients should keep taking it daily rather than expect the quick relief of a rescue inhaler. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which statement about allergen avoidance and the development of asthma is accurate?","choices":["Strict avoidance in infancy prevents asthma","Avoidance doubles the risk of atopic dermatitis","Avoidance cures established asthma","Avoidance does not reduce the risk of developing asthma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Inhaled allergens are common triggers and are implicated in sensitization, yet avoiding them does not reduce the risk of developing asthma; early exposure may even be protective by building tolerance. Avoidance still helps patients who already have asthma by removing triggers. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Airway hyper-responsiveness in asthma is best described as which response?","choices":["Loss of elastic recoil that collapses airways on expiration","Excessive bronchoconstriction to a trigger a normal airway ignores","Fixed narrowing that does not change with any trigger","Bronchodilation that overshoots after exposure to a trigger"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hyper-responsiveness is excessive bronchoconstriction in response to a trigger that would have no effect on a normal airway, and the degree of hyper-responsiveness is linked to how often symptoms occur. The key elements are a trigger, an exaggerated response and a normal airway that would not react. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which body mass index is linked to more frequent and harder-to-control asthma?","choices":["Below 18","Between 18 and 25","Between 25 and 27","Above 30"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Asthma is more frequent in people with a body mass index above 30, and their symptoms are more difficult to control; the mechanism is unknown. Obesity is listed among the endogenous risk factors with genetics, atopy and early viral infection. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which pain medication may be used by a patient with aspirin-sensitive asthma?","choices":["Naproxen","Indomethacin","Celecoxib","Ibuprofen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Patients with aspirin-sensitive asthma should avoid non-selective anti-inflammatory drugs (diclofenac, ibuprofen, naproxen, indomethacin) but may use selective agents such as celecoxib or meloxicam. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"How many exacerbations requiring oral corticosteroids in the past year may classify a patient as persistent despite minimal impairment?","choices":["0","1","2 or more","5 or more"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Patients with 2 or more exacerbations requiring oral systemic corticosteroids in the past year may be considered the same as patients with persistent asthma, even without the impairment levels of persistent disease. The risk domain can override a quiet impairment picture. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which form of tobacco exposure has evidence as an asthma risk factor?","choices":["Maternal smoking","Smoking by a relative who lives elsewhere","Nicotine replacement patches","Smoking that began after age 60"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Under passive cigarette smoke there is evidence that maternal smoking is a risk factor for asthma, although its effect is difficult to separate from the increased risk of respiratory infections in exposed children. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which feature is found in intrinsic but not extrinsic asthma?","choices":["Negative skin tests, normal immunoglobulin E","Attacks set off by allergen exposure","A different underlying airway pathology","Onset at the peak age of 3 years"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Intrinsic asthma, about 10% of patients, has negative skin allergy tests and a normal serum immunoglobulin E, starts later in adulthood, and is usually more severe and persistent. Extrinsic asthma is allergen-triggered and atopic. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 34, 35"},{"q":"What does a chest radiograph usually show in asthma?","choices":["Lobar consolidation","Pleural effusions","Normal lungs or hyperinflation","Bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The chest radiograph in asthma is usually normal or may show hyperinflation from air trapping, so it supports rather than makes the diagnosis and mainly helps exclude other causes of symptoms. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"At what time of day do asthma symptoms tend to peak?","choices":["3 to 4 in the morning","Noon","Late afternoon","Early evening"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms may be worse at night because of circadian variation in bronchomotor tone and bronchial reactivity, which peaks around 3 to 4 in the morning. Night awakenings are therefore one of the severity measures. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Exercise-induced bronchospasm typically peaks how long after exercise?","choices":["1 to 2 minutes","10 to 15 minutes","60 minutes","4 to 6 hours"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Exercise-induced bronchospasm begins during or within 3 minutes after exercise, peaks in 10 to 15 minutes and resolves by 60 minutes. That short, predictable window is why a short-acting inhaler before exercise works. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Which three components establish the diagnosis of asthma?","choices":["Symptom response to an oral corticosteroid trial","History, physical exam, and objective lung function","History, chest radiograph, and allergy testing","Physical exam, serum immunoglobulin E, and sputum culture"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Asthma is diagnosed from the history, the physical examination and an objective measurement of lung function. The objective measure matters because the exam can be normal between attacks, and it feeds both the diagnostic criteria and the severity classification. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Compared with beta-2 agonists, how do inhaled anticholinergics such as tiotropium perform in asthma?","choices":["More effective, with fast onset","Equally effective, with heavy side effects","Equally effective as rescue drugs","Less effective, with slow onset"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Anticholinergics such as tiotropium inhibit only the cholinergic reflex component of bronchoconstriction and mucus secretion, so they are less effective than beta-2 agonists and slow in onset; little systemic absorption leaves few side effects. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Which examination finding correlates with the degree of airway obstruction in asthma?","choices":["A pericardial friction rub","Inspiratory crackles at the bases","A shortened expiratory phase","A prolonged expiratory phase"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A prolonged expiratory phase during normal respiration correlates with obstruction, because air takes longer to leave through narrowed airways. In severe exacerbations wheezing may disappear, leaving decreased breath sounds with prolonged expiration. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which set of symptoms is typical of asthma?","choices":["Constant fever, night sweats, and weight loss","Pleuritic chest pain relieved by leaning forward","Stridor that is loudest on inspiration","Episodic wheezing, dyspnea, chest tightness, and cough"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Asthma symptoms vary but include episodic wheezing, dyspnea, chest tightness, cough and sputum production, occurring spontaneously or after a trigger and often worse at night. The episodic pattern reflects reversible obstruction. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which is an upper airway disorder in the differential diagnosis of asthma?","choices":["Vocal fold dysfunction","Bronchiectasis","Cystic fibrosis","Conversion disorder"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Upper airway mimics include vocal fold paralysis or dysfunction, foreign body aspiration and laryngotracheal masses; lower airway mimics include chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, bronchiectasis and cystic fibrosis. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"What is the key to success in asthma treatment?","choices":["Daily oral corticosteroids","Patient compliance","Allergen immunotherapy","Avoiding physical activity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The treatment goals are minimal chronic symptoms, no exacerbations or emergency visits, normal lung function and activity, and minimal drug side effects, and patient compliance is the key to reaching them. Non-compliance is also the most common reason for refractory symptoms. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What percentage of patients with asthma become worse with aspirin?","choices":["1 to 5%","10 to 15%","25 to 30%","50 to 60%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. About 1 to 5% of asthmatics become worse with aspirin, through a genetic predisposition that increases inflammatory mediators. Aspirin is among the medication triggers to avoid. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"On a written asthma action plan, the green zone corresponds to a peak flow of at least what percent of personal best?","choices":["50%","60%","80%","95%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The green zone, doing well, is a peak flow of 80% or more of personal best with long-term controllers taken daily; the yellow zone is 50% to 79%, when quick-relief medicine is added, and the red zone is below 50%, a medical alert. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 88, 90"},{"q":"Which statement about asthma across the life span is accurate?","choices":["Remission in adolescence is permanent","It worsens in severity with each decade of life","It may remit in adolescence and return in adulthood","It cannot begin after childhood"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Asthma presents at any age with a peak at 3 years, may become asymptomatic during adolescence, and may return in adulthood. A teenager whose asthma went quiet is not cured and should still be asked about childhood symptoms later in life. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"When is bronchial provocation testing useful in suspected asthma?","choices":["When spirometry already shows reversibility","When the 1-second volume is below 50% predicted","During an acute exacerbation","When spirometry is not diagnostic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bronchial provocation with a methacholine or histamine challenge is useful when spirometry is not diagnostic; an exercise challenge is used if exercise-induced bronchospasm is suspected. It is not recommended when the forced expiratory volume in 1 second is below 65% predicted. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What change between morning and afternoon peak flow readings indicates poor asthma control?","choices":["5%","10%","20%","50%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A 20% change between morning and afternoon peak flow measurements, or a 20% change from day to day, indicates poor asthma control and should prompt a review of the treatment plan. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 60"}]};
